Today, I practiced basic commands of Linux.
LINUX COMMANDS:
1) ls - to list the contents of directory

2) mkdir - to create a folder
   eg: mkdir ESSCI_2026

3) mkdir dir{1..10} - to create multiple folder
   eg: results: dir1 dir2 dir3 ------ etc

4) pwd - present working directory, it tells which directory we are currently working on

5) cd - change directory, used to change the directory path

6) touch - to create a file
   eg: touch F{1..10}.txt
       (touch File_name.extension)

7) cd .. - Goes to the parent diectory
   eg: current path: ~/ESSCI_2026/Adv_c/assignment$ cd ..
       parent path: ~/ESSCI_2026/Adv_c

8) cd - -goes to the previous directory
   eg: current path: ~/ESSCI_2026/LI$ cd -
       previos path: /home/laasya555/ESSCI_2026

9) rmdir - to delete the directory, it deletes only the directories which are empty
   eg: rmdir C++

10) rmdir d1 d2 d3, rmdir dir{1..10} - these are used to delete multiple folders/directories

11) rm - used to delete files
   eg: rm F{1..10}.txt

12) rm -r - even if the directory is not empty, we can delete by using this function
   eg: rm -r Template

13) vi file.txt- It is like a notepad, used to enter information in the file

14) After the notepad is open, click "i" (INSERT) to write the information

15) Click "ESC, :, wq, enter" - to save and close the notepad

16) cat - used to display the contents of the file(notepad)
  eg: cat file.txt

17) head file.txt - displays the first 10 lines in the file(notepad)

18) tail file.txt - displays the last 10 lines in the file(notepad)

19) head -n - used to display only certain lines in the notepad
  eg: head -2 -displays only the first 2 lines
    tail -5 -displays only the first 5 lines

20) cp - used to copy and paste the files from one directory to another
  eg: laasya555@Laasyasree:~/ESSCI_2026/Adv_c/Template$ cp file.txt ~/ESSCI_2026/Adv_c/assignment

- There are 2 different directory path:
1) Absolute path : cd ~/ESSCI_2026/Adv_c/Template -directory if it is starting from home
2) Relative path : cd Adv_c/Template - starts from current working directory

- The path follows/applies only forward direction
